'''''Deutzia crenata''''' is a species of flowering shrub in the hydrangea family ([[Hydrangeaceae]]). It is native to Japan, where it is called ''unohana'' (卯の花, literally "flower of the Month of the Rabbit"), and where it is common and found throughout the country.<ref name="Ohwi">{{cite book |last=Ohwi |first=Jisaburo |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/bibliography/43786 |title=Flora of Japan |publisher=[[Smithsonian Institution]] |year=1965 |isbn= |location=Washington |page=512 |language=en |doi=10.5962/bhl.title.43786 |lccn=65062683 |oclc=182709297 }}</ref> Its natural habitat is in forest edges and cliffs.<ref name=Mikawa>[http://mikawanoyasou.org/data/utugi.htm ''Deutzia crenata''] (in Japanese), Flora of Mikawa</ref> It has been commonly cultivated outside its native range, and has [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalized]] from plantings in China and North America.<ref name=Mikawa/><ref>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=241000493 ''Deutzia crenata'' Flora of China]</ref>

''Deutzia crenata'' is a [[deciduous]] shrub. Its leaves are opposite, dentate, and rather thickened. In contrast to the similar ''[[Deutzia scabra]]'', its leaves are not notably Nita’s diamorphic and are found on short petioles throughout the length of the stem. In addition, all of the filaments of ''Deutzia crenata'' are toothed.<ref name=Ohwi/> In Japan, this species produces flowers from May to July.<ref name=Mikawa/>

A form with doubled flowers (forma ''plena'') is popular in gardens.<ref>[http://mikawanoyasou.org/data/sarasautugi.htm ''Deutzia crenata'' form. ''plena''] (in Japanese), Flora of Mikawa</ref>

